"""Original facilitation teaching and point-mapped questions."""
def build(c,m):
 return c(61,'Participatory Facilitation and Community Dialogue','Participatory Facilitation\nand Community\nDialogue','Prepare and review a fictional community dialogue with a bounded decision purpose, accessible participation, constructive disagreement and an accountable feedback route.',[
 m('Purpose power and participation','Specify a dialogue purpose and distinguish attendance from influence.',[
'Facilitation creates conditions for people to consider an issue together; it does not give the facilitator ownership of the decision. Start by identifying the decision, who has authority over it and what participants can still influence. A meeting announced after a budget is fixed cannot honestly promise control of that budget. It may still offer a useful opportunity to discuss implementation if that narrower purpose is explicit. OECD guidance connects participation design with the stage and scope of a public decision. Describe the available influence before selecting an attractive meeting technique. [S32]',
'A community contains different interests and relationships rather than a single voice. Ask which groups may be affected, who normally speaks, whose time is constrained and who may prefer another way to contribute. An invitation through one organisation can reach valuable knowledge but cannot establish representation of all residents. IACD practice standards make engagement and inclusion relevant professional concerns. Their 2018 origin should not be confused with later training dates or a claim that an academy course has been accredited. In a fictional preparation note, identify missing perspectives as an unresolved design question rather than inventing their views. [S31]',
'Power also operates through language, agenda setting, access to documents and control over the written account. A UK qualitative synthesis of research co-production describes structural and relational barriers to sharing power. Its mental-health research context is different from ordinary community dialogue, so use it to question assumptions rather than claim a universal effect. Invite corrections to the agenda and explain how disagreement will be recorded. A respected chair or large attendance does not itself establish equal influence. Identify who can authorise changes and the process for responding when participants question the scope. [R42, S32]',
'The learner task is preparation using invented people and circumstances, not recruitment. Avoid asking classmates to disclose a personal experience to prove that an issue matters. State a voluntary fictional alternative and a way to pause. A useful purpose note records the question, authority, open and closed decisions, participation options and the next feedback point. It makes promises small enough to keep and makes constraints visible before people invest time. Such a note helps expose tokenism, but the existence of a note cannot prove that participation was meaningful or that later development outcomes improved. [S31, S32]'],
'A committee has already selected a venue but invites residents to choose the session time. Its poster says residents will decide everything. A shift worker cannot attend the proposed meeting.',
'Write a purpose note naming the open decision, fixed constraint, decision owner, missing perspective and one alternative contribution route.',
'Name the session time as open and the venue as fixed; correct the poster. Offer an accessible route without claiming to represent the absent shift worker.', ['S32','S31','R42'],[
('State what participants can influence before choosing a method.','A venue is fixed but the meeting time remains open. What should the invitation promise?','Influence over the time with the venue constraint explained.',['Control of every budget decision.','A guarantee of unanimous support.','No explanation until after attendance.'],'Honest scope prevents a promise beyond the organiser\'s authority.'),
('Attendance does not establish decision influence.','Forty people attend a fictional dialogue. What is still unknown?','Whether their contributions influenced a decision.',['The count of attendees.','Whether the room contained people.','That all residents necessarily agreed.'],'A count measures attendance, not the use of input.'),
('One organisation cannot automatically represent every resident.','A facilitator invites only one association and calls it the whole community. What needs review?','Whose perspectives and access routes may be missing.',['Whether every absent person agrees.','How to remove all differing views.','Why representation never matters.'],'Membership and influence vary within a community.'),
('Agenda and recording practices distribute power.','Only the chair may change an agenda or correct the minutes. What concern follows?','Control over which contributions become part of the decision record.',['Proof that equal influence is assured.','A need to collect private histories.','An automatic causal development benefit.'],'Procedural control can exclude contributions even during discussion.'),
('A fictional alternative supports voluntary learning.','A learner is asked to disclose a personal complaint in class. What is appropriate?','Use an alternative invented scenario without requiring disclosure.',['Require names to validate learning.','Publish the complaint as evidence.','Recruit a real complainant to replace the learner.'],'The course assesses reasoning without sensitive personal material.')]),
 m('Accessible dialogue and disagreement','Plan accessible discussion that preserves differences without forcing consensus.',[
'Accessibility starts before the meeting. Consider timing, travel, cost, physical access, language, hearing, reading demands and access to a private device. These are questions to check with relevant participants through approved arrangements, not stereotypes about a group. A digital channel can widen access for some while excluding others. OECD participation guidance therefore supports choosing a suitable mix of routes rather than treating online response as universally available. In a fictional exercise, explain which barrier a proposed adjustment addresses, who will arrange it and what remains untested. [S32]',
'Prepare plain information about the question and constraints so people do not need specialist vocabulary to contribute. Allow enough time to understand options and ask questions. Where interpretation or communication support is needed, plan appropriate competence and privacy arrangements. Do not assume a relative can safely translate every concern. The facilitator can invite a variety of contributions without demanding that each person speak publicly. IACD inclusion principles help frame this responsibility, while actual local accessibility and safeguarding requirements require qualified review. A checkbox saying accessible is less useful than a specific feasible arrangement. [S31, S32]',
'Disagreement is not automatically a failed process. It can reveal competing needs, uncertain evidence or a choice that disadvantages some people. Establish respectful discussion expectations, invite reasons and summarise differing positions without naming sensitive experiences. A facilitator should avoid humiliating a speaker, diagnosing motives or turning a complaint into a public investigation. The co-production synthesis suggests that negotiation can be uncomfortable even within valued relationships; it does not show that all conflict is productive. If a discussion becomes unsafe, pause and use the designated qualified response arrangements rather than promise that facilitation can resolve every issue. [R42, S31]',
'Record both common ground and unresolved differences in language participants can check. If agreement is required, clarify the approved decision rule rather than invent a vote halfway through the session. Silence may reflect uncertainty, language barriers or a choice not to speak, so it is not consent. Small-group options can help some people but still need attention to dominance and reporting accuracy. The fictional task tests a reasoned design, not real conflict mediation competence. Keep personal histories and identifiable accounts out of learner submissions and allow a different invented exercise when needed. [S32, S31]'],
'A dialogue uses only a phone app and declares agreement when nobody objects. Two invented residents share a device and another needs interpretation.',
'Propose two access arrangements, a way to check the recorded differences and a boundary for unsafe disagreement.',
'Offer feasible non-digital and communication options. Record silence as no stated contribution, not consent, and retain unresolved positions for the authorised decision process.', ['S32','S31','R42'],[
('Digital access is not universal access.','A fictional team accepts only app responses. What should it examine?','Who lacks a usable device, connectivity or a suitable alternative.',['Whether app use proves inclusion.','How to erase non-digital preferences.','Whether all residents own private phones.'],'Channel choice can create unequal access.'),
('Access arrangements need a barrier and an owner.','A plan says only make the dialogue accessible. What improves it?','A specific barrier, feasible adjustment and responsible organiser.',['A smaller font to fit more text.','A claim that no checking is needed.','An assumption about every disabled person.'],'Concrete arrangements can be reviewed and tested.'),
('Silence is not consent.','Nobody objects aloud to a fictional proposal. What is justified?','No objection was stated; agreement still needs an appropriate check.',['Every person freely consented.','All absent residents voted yes.','The proposal is legally binding.'],'Silence has multiple possible explanations.'),
('Record unresolved differences rather than force consensus.','Two participants give incompatible but relevant priorities. What should a facilitator do?','Clarify and record both reasons for the authorised decision process.',['Delete the less popular reason.','Declare one speaker irrational.','Require a personal disclosure to settle it.'],'A faithful account can preserve disagreement.'),
('Unsafe disagreement may require a qualified route.','A fictional meeting raises a safeguarding concern. What boundary applies?','Pause as appropriate and use designated qualified arrangements.',['Investigate the allegation publicly.','Guarantee safety through a discussion rule.','Require the class to decide guilt.'],'Facilitation does not confer safeguarding or adjudication authority.')]),
 m('Feedback and reflective improvement','Design a feedback record that distinguishes contribution from decision and outcome.',[
'Participation continues after people leave a room. Agree when a response will be given, who will prepare it and how people can access it without another costly visit. Explain which ideas were accepted, modified, deferred or declined, with reasons and the relevant decision owner. A summary of attendance is not feedback about how contributions were used. OECD guidance emphasises using input and closing the feedback loop. A fictional response can acknowledge a resource constraint without implying that contributors were wrong to raise an issue or that an unfunded promise is approved. [S32]',
'Check the accuracy of the account through a proportionate route before treating it as a settled community position. People should be able to correct a misrepresented contribution without supplying a personal history in a public form. Keep necessary status information separate from sensitive complaints and from learner work. A public summary may explain decisions in aggregate while a designated qualified role handles a private concern. IACD standards include learning and improving practice, but an improvement plan should name who will act and when it will be reviewed rather than merely celebrate a completed event. [S31]',
'Process review asks whether the purpose was clear, access arrangements worked, differing contributions were represented and feedback was delivered. An outcome review asks a different question about what changed for people or a service. Neither satisfaction nor attendance alone proves inclusion, accountability or development outcomes. A qualitative synthesis can inform questions about power, but its themes cannot certify that a particular dialogue shared authority successfully. Review missing perspectives, limits in the record and possible alternative explanations before claiming benefit. Use only invented aggregate examples here; actual evaluation requires appropriate governance and skills. [R42, S32]',
'A reflective facilitator can distinguish a controllable practice change from a dependency on another authority. Changing the timing of future sessions may be within the organiser\'s remit; allocating public funds may not be. State the owner of each proposed action and a realistic response if the dependency remains unresolved. Do not rename a proposal as implementation or an email as completed action. An introductory course offers practice in careful reasoning, not evidence that the learner has facilitated a real community process. Supervised observation, local review, accessibility testing and piloting remain separate conditions. [S31, S32]'],
'A committee reports a successful dialogue from thirty attendance slips, but no response to proposals has been sent. One proposed action requires another authority\'s approval.',
'Draft a feedback entry with input, decision status, reason, owner, next date and a separate process-review question.',
'Distinguish attendance from influence and a proposed action from approval. A useful entry assigns feedback responsibility and admits the unresolved dependency.', ['S32','S31','R42'],[
('Feedback explains the use of contributions.','A fictional response lists only attendance. What is missing?','How proposals were handled, why, and by whom.',['The names of private complainants.','A claim of unanimous consent.','A larger attendance count invented later.'],'Feedback concerns decisions and reasons, not only presence.'),
('Proposed actions differ from approved actions.','A budget request awaits an external authority. What status is accurate?','Proposed and dependent on approval.',['Fully implemented.','Guaranteed funded.','Certified by a classroom worksheet.'],'Status must reflect actual authority and evidence.'),
('Process quality differs from development outcomes.','Participants liked a fictional meeting. What cannot be concluded from this alone?','That community development outcomes improved.',['That some participants reported satisfaction.','That a meeting occurred.','That feedback could be collected.'],'Satisfaction is a process measure, not causal outcome evidence.'),
('Correction routes support a faithful account.','A summary misstates an invented participant\'s priority. What is appropriate?','An accessible correction route with proportionate information.',['Require a public personal history.','Prevent corrections after drafting.','Treat the summary as automatically unanimous.'],'Records should be checkable without unnecessary exposure.'),
('Reflective actions need responsible owners.','A review recommends a new session time. What makes the recommendation actionable?','A responsible organiser, feasible arrangement and review date.',['A declaration that all inequalities ended.','No owner because it is a suggestion.','An unrelated professional certificate.'],'Responsibility and timing allow follow-through to be assessed.')])])
